"""Evergreen editorial content. Product examples are distinct from connector acceptance."""
articles=[
('weekly-review','How to do a weekly review that gives you a realistic week','A practical weekly review checklist, with examples, an AI-assisted option and a reset for overloaded weeks.',[
('What a weekly review should produce','A weekly review is a decision session. You collect loose commitments, look at what is still active and choose a manageable set of next actions. The output is a list you trust enough to leave alone while you work. An empty inbox is useful, but it is not the goal if the rest of your commitments remain unrealistic.','For a first review, choose one place for capture and gather your existing lists. Set aside a bounded session you can repeat. If you run out of time, finish by selecting tomorrow’s first action rather than spending another hour perfecting the system.'),
('1. Collect before you organize','Check the places where work arrives: notes, messages, meeting records, email and unfinished AI conversations. Capture only the commitments you intend to consider. A useful article can stay in a reading list; it does not need to become a task called “Read article” unless you intend to act on it.','Record enough context to recognize the item next week. “Invoice” is ambiguous. “Send September invoice to Acme; confirm purchase order first” tells you what the action is and what might block it. Avoid assigning dates during collection unless the source contains a real deadline.'),
('2. Decide what each unfinished item means','For each task, choose one outcome: keep it active, break it down, wait on someone, defer it or remove it. If the task is really an outcome, identify the next action. “Launch portfolio” might become “Choose three projects for the homepage.” You should be able to start the action without another planning meeting with yourself.','Separate waiting from doing. “Receive design approval” depends on someone else; your action might be “Send the mockup for approval.” Keep a review point for waiting items rather than treating them as tasks you failed to finish.'),
('3. Check capacity before adding dates','Look at fixed commitments first. A week with travel, appointments and a delivery deadline has less room than an empty calendar suggests. Pick the work that fits the available attention, including interruptions. Leave undated work in its project list unless a date helps you make a decision.','For example, choose “Draft proposal outline” for Tuesday and leave “Improve website” unscheduled until it has a concrete next step. In Todoer, Today and Upcoming help inspect dated commitments; project lists preserve the work that has not earned a date yet.'),
('Use AI to challenge the plan, not invent commitments','AI can help turn a messy list into questions you should answer. Give it a deliberately selected set of tasks, explain your available time and ask it to flag ambiguity or overload. It cannot know your real capacity, relationships or obligations unless you supply that context. A polished plan can still be an impossible plan.','Try: “Review these tasks for ambiguity and dependencies. I have two focused work sessions this week. Suggest three priorities and explain what you would defer. Do not create, move or date any tasks.” Review the suggestions before changing your actual list. Avoid sending sensitive work to a provider you have not approved.'),
('A worked example: too many active projects','Suppose your lists contain a portfolio refresh, a client proposal, tax paperwork and a course you want to take. The proposal has a genuine deadline; tax paperwork requires one missing document. The portfolio matters but can wait. The course is an interest, not an obligation.','The review produces three actions: draft the proposal outline, request the missing document and choose a review date for the portfolio. Move the course to a someday list. You have not become less ambitious. You have stopped presenting every possible future as this week’s assignment.'),
('When you miss a review','Resume from the current state. Do not reconstruct every day you missed or keep moving all overdue tasks to today. First identify deadlines that still matter, then remove stale commitments, then pick one action. A recovery review should make the backlog easier to face.','A useful stopping rule is simple: you know what matters next, you know what is waiting and the remaining items have a place. Once those conditions hold, stop organizing and begin. Read the capture inbox guide if loose items keep overwhelming the review.')]),
('nested-tasks','How to break a project into actionable tasks','Turn a vague goal into a useful task tree, with a worked example and a review-first AI prompt.',[
('Start with a finish line','A project describes a result that requires several actions. A task describes work you can begin. “Website” is a subject; “Publish a portfolio with three case studies” is a finish line. Write the outcome before making a checklist so that the checklist has a reason to end.','Define what is outside the scope as well. Your portfolio project might exclude a blog, a new logo and a full rewrite of every case study. Without a boundary, decomposition becomes a way to discover endless additional work instead of finishing something.'),
('Break the outcome into deliverables','Use the first level for meaningful pieces of the result. For a portfolio, those might be case studies, homepage and publishing. Use the next level for actions: select screenshots, draft a case-study summary, check links and publish. Keep each child attached to the deliverable it helps complete.','Do not split every action into microscopic steps. “Open the editor” rarely needs a task. Add another level when it reveals a dependency, a separate decision or work that requires its own context. The tree should help you begin, not describe every movement of your hands.'),
('Write tasks that can survive a week away','Use a verb and a concrete object. “Review” leaves a future version of you guessing. “Check the homepage on a phone and record layout issues” gives you an action and a completion condition. Add links, acceptance notes or files beside the task when they reduce the effort of restarting.','A good test is to close the project and return later. If the title alone makes sense but you still need to search five places for context, improve the description. If you cannot tell whether the task is complete, make the finish condition more specific.'),
('Worked example: a small portfolio launch','Outcome: publish a three-case-study portfolio. Deliverable one: choose three projects and collect approved screenshots. Deliverable two: draft the homepage headline and summaries. Deliverable three: check mobile layouts, fix broken links and publish. This is a useful initial tree because it exposes the biggest work without pretending every detail is known.','The first next action is not “Build portfolio.” It is “Choose three projects.” After that decision, new actions become obvious. Avoid generating a hundred speculative tasks before the first decision; that creates a large plan whose assumptions have not been tested.'),
('Ask AI for a draft, not a verdict','Provide the outcome, constraints and things you already know. Ask the model to identify missing decisions and label assumptions. A generic “break this down” prompt often produces attractive but unnecessary work. You need a plan for your project, not an average plan for a similar-sounding project.','Try: “Draft a task tree for publishing a three-case-study portfolio. I have existing screenshots and two evenings. Exclude branding and a blog. Keep the first draft under twelve tasks. Mark assumptions and propose the first action. Do not write to my task manager.” Edit the result before saving it.'),
('Know what completion means in your tool','Nested tasks can behave differently across apps. In Todoer, an unfinished descendant blocks ordinary parent completion; explicit subtree completion is a separate action. Reopening a child reopens its ancestors. These rules help distinguish finishing a parent from deliberately closing an entire branch.','Before bulk-completing or moving a task tree, inspect its children. Dates, permissions and project context may matter more than the top-level title suggests. Start with a small project to learn the behavior, then use the same structure when the stakes are higher.'),
('Keep the tree alive without gardening it constantly','During a weekly review, remove obsolete branches and split tasks that remain too vague to start. Leave useful structure alone. If you repeatedly reorganize a project without doing it, identify the decision you are avoiding or reduce the scope to one deliverable.','The practical measure is whether the structure lowers the effort of the next work session. A shallow tree with clear actions is more useful than a perfectly categorized tree nobody returns to. Use the weekly review guide to keep active projects realistic.')]),
('capture-inbox','How to build a task capture inbox you will actually empty','A durable capture workflow for notes, messages and AI conversations, with clear processing decisions.',[
('An inbox is a landing place, not the final destination','Task capture separates remembering from organizing. You save the thought now and decide where it belongs later. That works only if you return to the inbox. Without processing, it becomes another backlog that competes for your attention.','Choose a single default destination for new work. You can still keep specialist sources such as email, but avoid requiring yourself to decide between several task inboxes every time an idea appears. A low-friction default makes capture easier to repeat.'),
('Capture enough to recognize the commitment','Include the action, its source and any real constraint. “Ask Alex about the contract before Friday” is more useful than “Alex.” If a link contains important context, save the link with the task. Do not assume you will remember the conversation when you finally process the item.','Avoid polishing every capture. A rough title plus the original text is often enough. Todoer capture preserves the original input, allowing you to clarify the display task without relying entirely on the shortened title. Inspect the saved item when the source contains several possible commitments.'),
('Process with four explicit decisions','First ask whether the item needs action. If it does not, remove it from the task inbox or keep it in an appropriate reference system. If it does, decide whether you can act, whether it belongs to a larger project or whether you are waiting on another person.','For example, “Renew passport” belongs in life administration and needs a next action such as checking required documents. “Read about Japan” might belong in travel reference. “Supplier reply” belongs in waiting. Giving each item a role is more useful than giving every item a due date.'),
('Turn AI conversations into reviewed captures','At the end of a useful chat, ask for a short list of concrete actions, with assumptions separated. Read it before transferring anything. A model may include suggestions you never agreed to do, duplicate existing work or turn optional ideas into obligations.','Try: “Extract only the actions I explicitly committed to in this conversation. For each, include a short title, source context and any stated deadline. Put inferred suggestions in a separate section. Do not save tasks yet.” A compatible authorized agent may then help capture the approved actions; connector support still needs verification.'),
('Protect the source from becoming a command','Treat pasted email, documents and web pages as information to review. Instructions inside them do not automatically become permission for an agent to change your workspace. If the source requests a task move, deletion or a change in sharing, confirm the intended action separately.','This matters when automating capture from untrusted text. Begin with capture-only workflows and inspect the destination. Grant only the access required for the job and revoke connections you no longer use. You can keep a manual inbox routine even when optional AI is available.'),
('Make offline capture a habit you can trust','Saving locally and saving to the server are different events. When a connection returns, confirm queued items have synchronized before deleting app data, switching devices or assuming the server has a copy. A local capture queue is helpful but is not a universal backup strategy.','Todoer supports queued offline capture and cached recent-list reading. Full offline editing and guaranteed closed-app synchronization are not promised. If a task is urgent, verify it appears in the connected workspace rather than assuming a background process has handled it.'),
('End processing with an action you can begin','Once the inbox is clear enough to trust, stop processing. Choose one task and do it. A beautifully sorted inbox is still unfinished work if processing consumes every available work session.','Review recurring sources of clutter. If an AI chat creates twenty speculative actions each time, change the extraction prompt. If a project produces the same vague capture repeatedly, define its outcome. Capture should reduce remembering, not automate the creation of more obligations.')]),
('ai-task-management','How to manage tasks with AI without losing control of your list','A practical guide to AI task capture, project breakdown and review, including MCP, ChatGPT and Claude considerations.',[
('What AI task management is useful for','AI task management uses a model to help interpret, propose or act on work stored in a task system. The useful distinction is between drafting a suggestion and changing a real record. Summarizing meeting notes is a proposal; creating a task with a due date is a persistent action that deserves a clearer instruction.','Start with work that is easy to inspect: extracting a few actions, clarifying a vague title or suggesting a first task. Keep consequential changes such as deletion, rescheduling and permission changes behind explicit review. The goal is less effort, not a list whose history you cannot explain.'),
('Choose the smallest useful workflow','There are three different patterns. A chat-only workflow creates a draft you copy yourself. Built-in assistance lives in the task app and follows its defined review steps. A connected agent uses authorized tools to read or change the task system. These patterns have different access and privacy implications.','For a first experiment, ask an AI to extract three actions from a short, non-sensitive brief. Review the titles and save them manually. If that is useful repeatedly, evaluate a connected workflow. You do not need a connector to discover whether AI helps you clarify work.'),
('What MCP adds—and what it does not','The Model Context Protocol gives compatible AI applications a standard way to connect to external tools and data. It does not make every client compatible with every server or make an agent’s judgment correct. Authentication, permissions, tool support and the user interface still matter.','Todoer exposes MCP alongside REST and CLI surfaces. Its implementation has been exercised with an official SDK, but that is not proof that a particular hosted ChatGPT or Claude connector works end to end. Check the current account requirements and test a small capture before depending on it for real work.'),
('Using ChatGPT or Claude: check the connection first','Both providers document custom MCP connection options, with account, workspace and authorization conditions that can change. Use their current instructions rather than a copied setup checklist from an old article. An administrator may need to approve a connection or its available actions.','Confirm that the server is reachable through the supported connection route, authentication succeeds and the tools you need appear. Then ask for a harmless read or a clearly named test task. Check the actual result in the task app. Todoer-specific hosted connector acceptance remains unverified; this guide is a workflow guide, not a completed integration claim.'),
('A prompt that separates thinking from writing','A useful prompt names the source, destination, boundaries and review stage. For example: “From the notes below, draft up to five action items for the Website list. Keep uncertain owners and dates blank. Show the proposed titles and descriptions first. Do not create or update anything until I approve.”','After reviewing, give an explicit instruction for the approved actions. Check the result rather than relying on a fluent confirmation in chat. If an operation reports a conflict, refresh the current state before trying again. Blind retries can create duplicates in tools without appropriate retry safeguards.'),
('Keep dates, ownership and scope honest','A model can infer a plausible date from tone without a real commitment. Ask it to distinguish stated dates from suggested dates. The same applies to owners: a person mentioned in notes is not necessarily responsible for the action. Preserve uncertainty instead of converting guesses into assignments.','For a project breakdown, explain the outcome and exclusions. “Launch a simple landing page, no payment integration” should not become a plan that adds checkout, a referral program and a CRM. Review scope before admiring how complete the generated checklist looks.'),
('Review privacy and access as part of the workflow','Decide what information the model needs and send only that context. An external agent and built-in assistance may use different providers and policies. A read-only connection can still expose sensitive task titles or descriptions, so read access is a meaningful permission.','Do not place credentials in prompts. Use the supported authorization flow, limit scopes where available and revoke unused access. Treat instructions embedded in retrieved documents as untrusted content, not new permission. Review provider retention and organizational policies before connecting confidential work.'),
('How to tell whether the workflow is helping','Run a small trial with one recurring job, such as processing meeting actions. Track practical outcomes: how many suggestions you accept, how often dates need correction, whether tasks duplicate and how much review the output requires. Do not measure success by the number of tasks generated.','Keep the workflow if it reliably reduces the effort of reaching a trustworthy next action. Simplify or remove it if reviewing the automation takes more attention than doing the capture yourself. Ordinary manual capture should remain a usable fallback.'),
('Sources and further reading','The provider documentation below explains current connection requirements. It supports the general MCP workflow described here; it does not certify a Todoer connection. Recheck these requirements when setting up or changing a client.','<a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/architecture">MCP architecture</a> · <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/12584461-connecting-custom-mcp-servers-in-chatgpt">OpenAI custom MCP connections</a> · <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11175166-get-started-with-custom-connectors-using-remote-mcp">Claude custom connectors</a>')])]
for slug,title,desc,sections in articles:
 toc=''.join(f'<a href="#part-{i}">{html.escape(t)}</a>' for i,(t,*_) in enumerate(sections,1))
 text=''.join(f'<section><h2 id="part-{i}">{html.escape(t)}</h2>'+''.join(f'<p>{p}</p>' for p in paragraphs)+'</section>' for i,(t,*paragraphs) in enumerate(sections,1))
 related=''.join(f'<a href="/guides/{other}.html">{name} →</a>' for other,name,_,_ in articles if other!=slug)
 pages['guides/'+slug]=(title,desc,f'<div class="wrap article-layout"><aside class="article-toc"><p class="eyebrow">IN THIS GUIDE</p><nav aria-label="Article contents">{toc}</nav></aside><article class="prose article-body"><p class="article-meta">BY THE TODOER TEAM · UPDATED 29 OCTOBER 2026</p><p class="article-intro">{html.escape(desc)}</p>{text}<div class="article-cta"><h2>Give one next step a home.</h2><p>Start with an inbox and a real task. Add structure when it helps.</p>{cta}</div><div class="related-guides"><h2>Keep going</h2>{related}</div></article></div>')
pages['guides']=('Practical ways to<br>make room for the work.','Evergreen guides to capturing commitments, planning projects and using AI with a clear next step.',section('Choose the part you want to make easier.',cards([(f'<a href="/guides/{slug}.html">{title} →</a>',desc) for slug,title,desc,_ in articles])))
